before:
Here is a complete TypeScript example for a TypeScript runner:
```typescript
interface Timed {
  title: string;
  minutes: number;
}
type Drawing = Timed & { kind: 'draw'; pictures: number };
type Singing = Timed & { kind: 'sing'; songs: number };
type Session = Drawing | Singing;
const drawing: Session = {
  kind: 'draw', title: 'Birds', minutes: 12, pictures: 3
};
const singing: Session = {
  kind: 'sing', title: 'Warmup', minutes: 8, songs: 2
};
```
Each record needs Timed's title and minutes plus its own kind and detail. & combines requirements; it does not merge two runtime objects. | says a Session is one of the two valid shapes.

Continue after the previous types and objects:
```typescript
function describeSession(session: Session): string {
  if (session.kind === 'draw') {
    return `${session.title}: ${session.pictures} pictures.`;
  }
  return `${session.title}: ${session.songs} songs.`;
}
console.log(describeSession(drawing));
console.log(describeSession(singing));
```
The outputs are Birds: 3 pictures. and Warmup: 2 songs. === tests exact equality. The if branch returns early, so the second return is reached only for the singing shape. Trying to read songs in the drawing branch is a type error.

after:
Both sessions need a title. The kind label tells you which extra property belongs to that session.
```typescript
interface Titled { title: string; }
type Drawing = Titled & { kind: "draw"; pictures: number };
type Singing = Titled & { kind: "sing"; songs: number };
type Session = Drawing | Singing;
function describeSession(session: Session): string {
  if (session.kind === "draw") {
    return `${session.title}: ${session.pictures} pictures.`;
  }
  return `${session.title}: ${session.songs} songs.`;
}
console.log(describeSession({ kind: "draw", title: "Birds", pictures: 3 }));
```
The output is Birds: 3 pictures.
